  <h3>Folder access on Android, settled</h3>

  <p>Two authoritative-looking sources contradicted each other. The contradiction is resolved:
  folder access shipped on Chrome for Android in version 132, confirmed by the vendor's own
  release records and by an engineer on the implementation thread. The compatibility database
  that reports otherwise is stale.</p>

  <p>Two caveats survive. The embedded-browser variant used inside native applications did not
  ship and remains disabled. And four defects filed at launch — a picker that freezes on large
  photo folders, saving that only offers to overwrite rather than name a new file, ignored file
  filters, and an error on one save path — appear to be unresolved; a developer was still
  asking after their status eight months later without an answer.</p>

  <h3>Safari, in detail</h3>

  <p>Safari is the most constrained desktop target and the least verified. The write interface
  used by the staging path did not exist until Safari 26, released September 2025; earlier
  versions require a materially more complex worker-based path. Private windows disable the
  storage entirely. Against that, the quota is generous — about 77 GB on desktop and 38 GB on
  iPhone — and far exceeds Firefox's default.</p>

  {{ c.warn('Seven-day expiry',
     'Safari deletes data a site created after seven days of browser use without a visit to
      that site. Chromium and Firefox do not. Whether this matters depends entirely on how long
      a paused download may realistically sit before someone returns to it — a product
      question that has not been settled, and one this report deliberately does not answer.') }}

  <h3>Mobile, briefly</h3>

  <p>On iPhone and iPad, JavaScript is suspended almost immediately once the browser is sent to
  the background, and downloads are reported as cancelled when the user switches applications.
  Keeping the screen awake does not help: that facility prevents the screen dimming and does
  nothing once another application comes to the front. The practical ceiling on those devices
  is around 100 MB, and the failure mode is the page dying without any error the application
  can catch.</p>
